>> A2: What is the maximum size of a file supported by your inode
>> structure?  Show your work.
Each inode contains:
 -> uint32_t file_or_dir (4 bytes)
 -> off_t length (4 bytes)
 -> unsigned magic (4 bytes)
 -> block_sector_t direct[NUM_DIRECT] (4 * 123 = 492 bytes)
 -> block_sector_t indirect (4 bytes)
 -> block_sector_t double_indirect (4 bytes)

Total of 512 bytes for each inode.

Each indirect block is one full sector, so it has 512 / 4 = 128 block ptrs 

So we have 123 direct blocks,
128 indirect blocks,
128 x 128 = 16384 double indirect blocks

Total of 123 + 128 + 16384 = 16635 addressable blocks

BLOCK_SECTOR_SIZE = 512 bytes/block 

So, 16635 * 512 = 8517120 bytes 

8517120 / (1024 * 1024) = roughly 8mb

So max file sizes is roughly 8mb or exactly 8517120 bytes

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A3: Explain how your code avoids a race if two processes attempt to
>> extend a file at the same time.

To prevent a race if two processes attempt to extend a file at the 
same time, we implemented an inode-level lock (every inode has a 
lock), 'inode_lock'. 

Extending files occurs in inode_write_at when offset + size 
exceeds the current file length. If this criteria is met,
the current thread acquires the lock and any other thread
trying to extend the file must wait before the current 
thread releases the lock.

