---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B2: Describe your code for traversing a user-specified path.  How
>> do traversals of absolute and relative paths differ?

The code for traversing a user-specified path is basically all done
in a helper function of char * traverse_path (const char *name, struct dir **directory).
First of all it starts by seperating absolute bs realtive paths. If the path starts with
a / then it is an absolute path and as such we start at the root directory. On the other
hand if it doesn't start with a / then it is a realtive path and we start with the 
processes/threads current directory which is stored as a paramater in the thread struct.
From there using the strtok_r method as explained in string.c we can break our path into
tokens seperated by the / delimiter. This is very convenient as each token would be the next
directory we have to go to. Then in a while loop we use strtok_r to continue on the
subdirectory path by using dir_lookup on the current subdirectory so we can go to the next 
subdirectory. We repeat this until we have the final token as the final token in the path 
would be the possible filename or dirname to create,open or remove (i.e a/b/c/d d can be 
a filename to create, it is not necessarily a directory). So once we stop at the last token
which is a name for a directory or file we return it to do whatever we need. Also when going
through subdirectories to reach our path destiniation we also note that we implemented the '.'
and '..' special names/directory entries. '.' representing the current directory while
'..' represents the parent directory. Also because we use stringtok_r method unconventional,
but acceptable paths such as a//b would work as well, behaving the same way as a/b.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B4: How do you prevent races on directory entries?  For example,
>> only one of two simultaneous attempts to remove a single file
>> should succeed, as should only one of two simultaneous attempts to
>> create a file with the same name, and so on.

Before performing any directory operations, the current thread 
acquires the directory's inode_lock. This prevents all possible 
race conditions. For example, when two threads try to create a 
file with the same name simultaneously, both will call dir_add.
The first one to acquire the inode_lock scans the directory, 
finds the name free, inserts the new entry, and releases the 
lock. The second thread then acquires the lock, sees
the name already exists, and fails.

>> B5: Does your implementation allow a directory to be removed if it
>> is open by a process or if it is in use as a process's current
>> working directory?  If so, what happens to that process's future
>> file system operations?  If not, how do you prevent it?

Our implementation does not allow a directory to be removed if it is 
open by a process or if it is in use as a process's current working 
directory. 

Whenever a directory is opened (by dir_open, filesys_open, etc), 
it's inode's open_cnt field is incremented by 1, and every additional 
opener increases the count. During removal, the thread first acquires 
the inode_lock, then checks whether open_cnt == 0 first before removing 
the directory. If it's != 0, directory is still in use and removal fails 
with an error.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B6: Explain why you chose to represent the current directory of a
>> process the way you did.

We chose to represent the current directory of a process as a struct dir* cur_dir;
in the thread/processes thread struct. We represented it this way so that different
processes each have their own seperate current directory. This is because after 
exec is called and the child process inherits the parents directorythe two processes
directories are completely independent. So obviously storing the directories as struct dir*
was the most logical way to store a processes current directory. It is easy for a process 
to access (just need to do thread_current() to get thread/process paramters) and the struct 
dir obviously has everything a process needs as it is the struct designed for dirs. One important thing 
to mention is that when the child process inherits the parents directory we don't just set the directory 
as an equivalence with '='. If we did that then both processes would share the same directory no matter what
and they wouldn't be independent. So instead the child processes would use the dir_reopen function
to get their own independent directory that is still the same directory as the parent (this is in the sense 
that they have the same directory struct, but the pointers aren't the same so they can change them without
affecting one another). Once again because we use a struct dir* and because of the way the struct dir has an 
inode in it 2 processes can have the same directory info while not being stuck together (can move to 
different directories).



